%% Azure File Sync agent lifecycle on a Contoso registered server - the states a version passes through and the update policy that decides who moves it
%% Source: Microsoft Learn "Plan an Azure File Sync deployment", sections Azure File Sync agent update policy, Automatic management of the agent lifecycle, Agent lifecycle and change management guarantees, read 2026-09-03; assessor rules AFS-05, AFS-06, AFS-12, AFS-15 in docs/RULES.md
stateDiagram-v2
  direction TB

  state "Agent version on one registered server" as LIFE {
    direction TB
    state "Current - newest major version, for example 22.x" as CUR
    state "Supported - an older major still inside its window" as SUP
    state "Expiring - warning raised at least 3 months before expiry" as EXPG
    state "Self-update window - inside 21 days of the posted expiry date" as WIN
    state "Expired - connecting with this version is blocked and sync stops" as EXP
    state "Unregistered - server retired from the Storage Sync Service" as GONE

    [*] --> CUR
    CUR --> SUP : a newer major ships, at least 3 months of overlap
    SUP --> EXPG : metric StorageSyncAgentVersionExpirationDays rises above 0
    EXPG --> WIN : 21 days before the posted expiry date
    WIN --> CUR : self-update succeeds inside the maintenance window
    WIN --> EXP : every weekly attempt fails, for example no route to Microsoft Update
    EXPG --> CUR : operator updates with AfsUpdater.exe or the msp patch
    SUP --> CUR : operator takes the update early
    EXP --> CUR : reinstall a supported version - registration and server endpoints survive
    EXP --> GONE : server decommissioned instead of updated
  }

  note right of LIFE
    The guarantees Microsoft publishes, which is what makes this plannable.
    A major version is supported at least 12 months from its release.
    Consecutive major versions overlap by at least 3 months.
    Expiry warnings reach registered servers at least 3 months out.
    A minor version dies with its major - 18.2.0.0 expires when 18.0.0.0 does.
    Installing an expired agent warns but succeeds. Connecting with one is blocked.
    Downgrade is never supported - roll forward, never back.
  end note

  state "Update policy set per server" as POLICY {
    direction TB
    state "UpdateBeforeExpiration - the default guardrail" as P1
    state "InstallLatest with a chosen day and hour" as P2
    state "Manual only - clustered file servers" as P3

    [*] --> P1
    P1 --> P2 : Set-StorageSyncAgentAutoUpdatePolicy -PolicyMode InstallLatest -Day d -Hour h
    P2 --> P1 : Set-StorageSyncAgentAutoUpdatePolicy -PolicyMode UpdateBeforeExpiration
    P1 --> P3 : the server is a failover cluster node
    P2 --> P3 : flighting excludes clustered servers
  }

  note right of POLICY
    P1 attempts a self-update once a week, only inside the last 21 days,
    and only in the maintenance window. It does not replace Microsoft Update patching.
    P2 takes each flighted version as it becomes generally available.
    P3 is Contoso reality on the clustered file servers - assessor AFS-15 lists
    them so the per-node update is scheduled by hand.
    Two version floors Contoso design depends on.
    Managed identity for File Sync needs agent v20 or later - assessor AFS-12.
    The Software Assurance plus Azure Arc per-server discount needs v22 or later.
    The alert that drives all of this is StorageSyncAgentVersionExpirationDays
    greater than 0, evaluated hourly over a 12 hour lookback - 90 days of warning.
  end note
